  "markdown": "Guide\n\n# What to send for a wallpaper installation quotation\n\nBy [Dorin Burcus](https://www.mrwallcover.com/about/#dorin), founder · Last updated 22 October 2026\n\nStart with the location, the rooms and the finish you have in mind. Photographs, approximate dimensions and a product reference help us prepare for the survey; leave anything you do not yet know open.\n\n[Start your project](https://www.mrwallcover.com/contact/?intent=install) [See the work](https://www.mrwallcover.com/projects/)\n\nYou do not need a complete specification to start a conversation. Send what you know about the rooms and the material, and mark anything that still needs a decision. These notes help us prepare for the survey; they are not a substitute for seeing the walls.\n\n## The rooms and the location\n\n- The project location and which rooms or areas are included.\n- Approximate widths and heights, or current drawings if you have them. Say what has been measured and what is approximate.\n- Whether the room is a home, a hotel or another working building, and whether it will be occupied during the work.\n\nThe smallest commission we take on is a single room. We also take on [programmes that run for years](https://www.mrwallcover.com/services/#commission-scope).\n\n## The chosen wallcovering\n\n- The maker, collection, product code and colourway, with the hanging instructions where available.\n- Whether the paper is already ordered, whether it is from one batch and who supplies it.\n- What is still undecided. If you are choosing, the [materials library](https://www.mrwallcover.com/materials/) explains the families and what they ask of the room.\n\nThe product reference tells us more than a description: usable width, pattern repeat, trimmed or untrimmed edges, backing and any panel sequence all affect the work. A scenic panel needs its own set-out; it cannot be treated as an interchangeable roll.\n\n## Photographs and access\n\n- Photograph each wall square-on, including corners, doors, windows and any joinery the covering will meet.\n- For a staircase, show it from below and from the landing, with the ceiling height if known.\n- Include access constraints: lifts, common parts, parking, high walls or a stair void. Say what furniture and finished surfaces need protection.\n\n## Preparation and timing\n\n- Describe the existing paper or paint and any known damage, damp history or recent plastering. The [wall preparation guide](https://www.mrwallcover.com/advice/wall-preparation/) explains what needs inspection.\n- Give your preferred timing and any work that must finish first. Include permitted working hours or hotel room-release restrictions where relevant.\n\nPreparation may include stripping old paper, filling, sanding, priming or lining where the wall and the specification require it. What can only be confirmed after stripping or inspection should be recorded as uncertain, with a way to agree any extra work.\n\n## What the survey settles\n\nThe survey confirms the wall measurements, wall readiness, access and the material’s set-out needs. Openings are taken out of the labour measure; the purchase quantity separately allows for full drops, repeat and wastage. A large repeat, a drop match or a room broken up by openings can change both the material order and the time on site.\n\nAfter the survey we write a scope of works by room. The quotation says what has been allowed for, including access, protection and making good. Every job is quoted after survey; Mr Wallcover publishes no price list or flat rate per roll or square metre.\n\nAftercare is included: a return visit about four to six weeks after completion and twelve months’ cover on the workmanship of the hang. The [aftercare page](https://www.mrwallcover.com/aftercare/) explains the care advice and guarantee limits.\n\n## Understand the price factors\n\nThe full guide, [What changes the cost of wallpaper installation?](https://www.mrwallcover.com/advice/installation-cost/), explains measured area, preparation, material, pattern repeat, access and programme, and what a complete quotation should include. Use this checklist to prepare the enquiry and that guide to compare the scope behind the price.\n\nDiagram: Wall elevation showing overall width, height, an opening and a vertical datum. Labels: Width W; Height H; Door; True vertical.\n\nSchematic · not to scaleMeasured wall area, openings and the set-out inform the quotation. This drawing explains the survey; it is not a price or ordering calculation.\n\nQuestions\n\n## Questions we are asked\n\nIllustrative material studies\n\nPause motion\n\n### Why is there no price per roll?\n\nBecause the roll does not decide the labour. The same number of rolls can be a morning in an empty, flat room or several days on a staircase with a large pattern repeat, poor walls and a hand-trimmed paper. The quotation follows the survey.\n\n### How much extra paper should be allowed?\n\nThe allowance is typically 15–30%, depending on the pattern repeat and the layout, with staircases at the high end (around 30%) because of the angles.\n\n### When do I get a price?\n\nAfter the survey. We measure, look at the walls and the access, and then write a scope of works room by room, saying what has been allowed for. Drawings and a product code let us prepare before the visit.\n",
